## 1. Introduction
Halogenated anilines are crucial raw materials and synthetic intermediates for the production of pesticides, pharmaceuticals, dyes, and other fine chemicals [1-3]. The methods for the synthesis of halogenated anilines at early stage are always the noncatalytic process, using stoichiometric iron powder, alkali sulfide, or metal hydride as the reducing agents. Nevertheless, these reduction approaches have a number of shortcomings, including substantial environmental pollution, difficult separation of products, and high cost of reducing agents [4-6]. Therefore, extensive interests have been focused upon the catalytic reduction method due to their low pollution, low cost, and low energy consumption. In the catalytic reduction process of halogenated nitrobenzenes to corresponding anilines, the core problem is the development of highperformance catalysts. Thus, enormous efforts have been paid on developing catalysts with high activity, high selectivity, and high stability.
Although noble-metal-based catalysts, like Pd, Pt, Ir and Aubased catalysts, are usually applied for the catalytic hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes, their low reserve, high cost, and especially the poor selectivity when other reducible functional groups co-existed in one substrate, these disadvantages significantly impede their large-scale commercialization application to a great extent [7-10]. Nowadays, non-noble metal catalysts are explored as promising alternatives because of their low cost and potential practical applicability. In this context, Fe, as one of the cheapest and most widely distributed metals, is often used as the catalytic active sites. Especially, Fe–N–C catalysts which show good catalytic ability towards these reactions, making them one of the best candidates for the replacement of precious metals. Many efficient Fe–N–C catalysts with Fe or Fe oxide nanoparticle, nanocluster, and even single atom (SA) active sites have been reported [11-14]. However, the catalytic active centers of these catalysts are usually difficult to be determined, and the study and identification of the active sites is still a fascinating and hot issue. For example, Beller et al. reported that nanoscale $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ surrounded by an $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon layer showed unique catalytic activity for the hydrogenation of functionalized nitroarenes [15]. Yun and co-workers reported $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ nanoparticles encapsulated in $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon tubes were the active sites for the reduction of functionalized nitro compounds [9]. Li’s group revealed that isolated Fe SAs supported by $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon could lower the energy barrier and show superior catalytic hydrogenation performance of nitrobenzenes [12,16]. Thus, the inference and discussion on the active sites are still at the center of intense debate.
The main problem for the intensive research of Fe–N–C is its complicated and diversified catalyst composition, as Fe has a variety of valence $(\mathrm{Fe}^{0}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}{\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}.$ , multiple oxidation states, Fe-C composites and Fe-N compounds [13-18]. At the same time, the existing forms and pore structures of the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon carrier also have a significant influence on the catalytic performance [19-21]. Generally, researchers only focus on the study of the single existing Fe forms, and take them as the active sites in the catalysts, while ignoring other Fe species and influencing factors. Therefore, such studies are incomplete and not comprehensive, and it is difficult to have a deep understanding of the reaction mechanism. For Fe–N–C catalysts, the synergistic effect among various Fe components, the interaction between Fe species and the support at the interfaces, and the overall interacts and federates with each other may all play an integral role in the catalyst performance. Therefore, the study of the effect of each part on the overall catalytic performance of Fe–N–C catalysts is highly desirable.
Recently, Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a threedimension ordered crystal structure with rich micropores and high surface areas constructed by linking metal ions and ligands, provide a promising platform for the design of Fe–N–C catalysts with high performance [13,14,21,22]. Fe–N–C with various existing forms can be easily prepared by a calcination process using MOF as the precursor template consisted of C, N, and Fe source. Thus, by removing or covering one active center in a single catalyst, the single or synergistic effect of each component could be studied, which can effectively avoid the influence of other affecting factors. And it is well-known that the precise control of morphology, structure and composition of MOF is crucial for the catalytic performance of MOF-derived catalysts [23-25]. Herein, by simply adjusting the adding amount of vitamin C (VC), a series of Fedoped $Z\mathrm{IF-X}$ $\mathbf{\dot{x}}$ represents the adding millimole quantity of VC) with different morphologies (from cubic to truncated octahedral shape) was synthesized in one pot. After controllable annealing of the MOF precursor, single-atom Fe integrated with small Fe nanoclusters implanted in an $N$ -doped carbon layer were obtained $(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}–\mathrm{N}–\mathrm{C}-\mathbf{X})$ . Combining the advantages of Fe single-atom and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C,$ we surprisedly found that $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-N-C-}0.75$ exhibited excellent catalytic performance for the transfer hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzene even at room temperature, and the ratio of hydrazine hydrate $\left(\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}.\mathsf{H}_{2}0\right)$ to substrate could be kept at a comparatively low level. The active centers of the designed catalyst were determined by the poisoning tests and acid leaching experiments, which indicated that Fe single-atom and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ jointly promoted the catalyst to achieve better performance. Furthermore, density functional theory (DFT) calculations demonstrated the overall reaction barrier on $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ integrated with Fe single-atom was much lower than that on separated $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ or Fe single-atom.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O});$ , iron (III) acetylacetonate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{15}\mathrm{H}_{21}\mathrm{FeO}_{6}$ ), 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIM), 4- chloronitrobenzene (4-CNB), and other halogenated substrates were obtained from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. VC was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Company Limited. Ethanol $(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}0\mathrm{H})$ and hydrazine hydrate $\left(\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}.\mathsf{H}_{2}0\right)$ were provided by Tianjin Damao Chemical Reagent Factory. All chemicals were of analytical grade and directly used without further purification.
2.2. Preparation of Fe-doped ZIF–x, $F e_{S A}/F e_{N C}–N–C-X$ ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0}}$ , 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25 and 1.5) and other contrast materials
Fe-doped ZIF–x was prepared by a simple one-pot procedure: 2 mmol $Z_{\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ 2 mmol ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ and different mole quantity of VC (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25 and $1.5\mathrm{\mmol}$ ) were firstly dissolved in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water to form a homogenous solution with the help of ultrasonic wave. Then $80~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water containing 48 mmol 2-MeIM was quickly added into the former solution. The mixed solution system was stirred at room temperature overnight. The solid products (Fe-doped ZIF–x) were obtained by filtering, washed with deionized water several times and finally dried in a vacuum oven. When only 4 mmol $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.$ - $6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ was added without the addition of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ and VC, which denoted as ZIF was obtained. When only 4 mmol $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0.75\mathrm{~g~VC}$ without $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ were employed, ZIF with VC was acquired.
The $\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{NC}-\mathrm{N-C-X}}$ were obtained by directly annealing the MOF precursors $(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}–\mathrm{N}–\mathrm{C}-\mathbf{X})$ under an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere with a ramp rate of $3\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ at $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a tubular furnace, and cooled naturally to room temperature. With the same annealing condition as that used for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N-}\mathrm{C-}\mathrm{x,N-}$ C and $\mathsf{N-C-}0.75$ could be acquired by the pyrolysis of ZIF and ZIF with VC.
## 2.3. Procedure for the hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes
In a typical experiment, a certain amount of catalyst, $0.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ halogenated nitrobenzene, and $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol were added into a dried flask, then dispersed by ultrasound. After heating to a certain reaction temperature, different amount of ${\sf N}_{2}{\sf H}_{4}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ was quickly added to the reaction flask with vigorous magnetic stirring. Reaction samples were taken at a certain reaction time through a syringe, and filtered through a filter head. The reaction samples were analyzed using gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC–MS) to track the reaction process. After each experiment, the catalyst used in the reaction solution was recovered by centrifugation, washed with ethanol, and reused in a subsequent reaction test to verify the recycling ability of the catalyst.
## 2.4. Characterization and computational method
The characterization instruments used and the computational methods performed for DFT calculation are available in Supporting Information.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalysts preparation and characterization
The catalyst preparation procedure is schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. By adding different mole quantities of VC into the solution system when other contents were consistent, a series of Fe-doped ZIF– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ with different morphologies was synthesized in one pot. Typically, the target annealing product $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{X}$ was generated by the pyrolysis of certain Fe-doped ZIF–x at $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The morphology of the Fe-doped ZIF– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ precursor and the aimed product $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}–\mathsf{N}–\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ were characterized by utilizing transmission electron microscope (TEM). Interestingly, we found that the VC adding amount had a significant influence on the morphology of Fe-doped ZIF–x, and that in turn affected the shape of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N-C-}\mathrm{X}.$ . The images are shown in Fig. 2a–g, respectively. Without VC, Fe-doped ZIF–0 (Fig. 2 a1-a2) exhibited a crystal structure with a cubic-shape. When VC was added, the angle of the polyhedron was truncated by the removal of six right square pyramids. This phenomenon became more evident as the adding amount of VC increased. As a result, the Fe-doped ZIF–x with a shape like a truncated hexahedron or octahedron was obtained (Fig. 2 b1-g1, b2-g2). In order to display these evolution process more graphically, we made a simple graphical representation, as shown in Fig. 3.
The target $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ was obtained directly by the hightemperature calcination under an inert atmosphere of a certain Fedoped ZIF–x precursor. Concluded from the TEM images shown in Fig. 2 a3- $^{.{\bf g}3}$ , it indicated that the morphology of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathsf{C}-{\bf X}$ had a big difference, which was greatly influenced by the precursor or the adding amount of VC to some extent. The central core of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{X}$ partly inherited the original morphology of the precursor, but their outer surfaces were not the same. Intertwined carbon nanotubes that covered the central core could be found, and this phenomenon was more evident in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ (Fig. 2 d3). The brushy carbon nanotubes with Fe active sites planted on the outer surfaces could exhibit potential advantages in mass transfer, which may do good to the catalytic process. The hollow nanotubes were generated through a bottom-up catalytic strategy during the pyrolysis process due to the contribution of Fe and C species coming from the organic ligands. What’s more, a more detailed characterization of the representative catalyst $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}^{-}}$ N–C–0.75 was conducted, as $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ exhibited the best catalytic performance among the target samples. The scanning electron microscopy image and TEM images of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-C-$ 0.75 under different magnification were displayed in Fig. S1. Dark-field TEM (DF-TEM) images of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}{-\mathsf{N}}{-\mathsf{C}}{-\mathbf{X}}$ and the corresponding elemental mapping images are displayed in Fig. 2 a4-g4. As shown, relatively well-dispersion of C, N, Fe elements demonstrated their homogeneous presence, and some of the dense regions in Fe elements came from the agglomerating Fe species.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were performed to characterize the crystallinity structure and the phases present in the materials. Firstly, we compared the XRD patterns (Fig. S2) of the precursors ZIF, ZIF with VC, Fe-doped ZIF–0, and Fe-doped ZIF–0.75, since the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0.75$ derived from Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 exhibited the optimal catalytic performance. According to the XRD results, the precursors ZIF (without the addition of Fe $(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ and VC) and ZIF with VC showed high crystallinity and similar structure to ZIF-8. When $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ was added, the XRD patterns of Fe-doped ZIF–0 and Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 became complicated, but still remained the diffraction peaks like ZIF-8, which may indicate the good incorporation of Fe species into the ZIF structure. As the cavity diameter of ZIF-8 was $\bar{1}1.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and the molecular size of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ was $9.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ could come and reside into the cavity of ZIF cages with the assembly of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and 2-MeIM during the encapsulating process [23,26].
To detect the calcination characteristics and determine the calcined target temperature, Thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA) was used. As shown in Fig. 4, the VC showed a significant weight loss at about $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ after the loss of the physically absorbed water, which was attributed to the decomposition of VC structure. The TGA curves of ZIF and Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 were about the same, and they showed good stability which may be due to the strong coordination between imidazole ligands and metal species. With the increase in pyrolysis temperature, Zn species will evaporate [27,28]. Fe species doped in the ZIF structure will be reduced by VC and imidazole ligands to form different Fe active sites. Based on the TGA analysis and the complete removal of Zn species, $900~^{\circ}C$ was adopted for the preparation of the target catalysts. And the TGA behavior of the target annealing product $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}^{-}}$ $\mathsf{N-C-}0.75$ derived from Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 was also performed, which showed good stability and evidenced that this material was relatively stable when temperature was lower than $250^{\circ}\mathsf C.$
The XRD patterns corresponding to the annealed products $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}^{-}}\mathrm{N}_{-}\mathrm{C}_{-}\mathrm{x}$ were also measured. From Fig. 5, we can see that all the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{X}$ samples showed a broad peak at 2h around $26.2^{\circ}$ , which was attributed to the (00 2) plane of graphitic carbon [13,16,29]. Under the same pyrolysis conditions, $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N}{-\}}-0$ exhibited the smallest carbon peak. To distinguish the subtle differences corresponding to the carbon matrix, the Raman characterization of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ was also conducted, which was shown in Fig. S3. The two dominant bands at 1350 and
$1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to defective hybridized (D) and crystallized graphitic (G) carbon were present in the spectra, and the value of $I_{G}/I_{D}$ ratio reflected the structure defects of the samples [14,30]. The Raman characterization results revealed that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}^{-}}$
N–C–0.75 possessed more defects, which maybe one reason for its best catalytic performance. Meanwhile, the diffraction peaks assigned to $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ could be observed, revealing the abundant existence of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanoparticles [25,31,32]. Noticeably, differences existed between $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C}-0$ and other $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}–\mathsf{N}–\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ samples. The distinct peaks located at about $44.6^{\circ}$ , $64.7^{\circ}$ and $82.3^{\circ}$ assigned to the characteristic (1 1 0), (2 0 0) and (2 1 1) diffractions of metallic Fe (JCPDS no. 01–1262) [33] could be obviously observed in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0$ . And the peak intensity at 2h around $45.0^{\circ}$ was very high in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N}{-\}}-0$ . When VC was added, the diffraction peak at 2h around $45.0^{\circ}$ , and peaks attributed to (200) and (211) diffractions of Fe decreased compared to $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}^{-}}\mathrm{N}_{-}$ C–0. This may due to the peak at about $45^{\circ}$ was the superposition of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ and metallic Fe in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-N-C-}0,$ as these two Fe species were the main constituents in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}–\mathrm{N}–\mathrm{C}–0.$ . And in other $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}^{-}}\mathrm{N}_{-}\mathrm{C}_{-\mathrm{X}}$ samples, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ was the major component. Thus, it could be concluded that the addition of VC had a significant impact on the formation of Fe species. In addition, it was worth noting that small diffraction peaks corresponding to the (200) and (220) planes of iron nitride at $50.8^{\circ}$ and $74.6^{\circ}$ were identifiable in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\mathrm{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}0$ , which were the characteristic peaks for Fe–N moieties (JCPDS no. 75–2127) [34,35]. However, the peaks of Fe–N species in some $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}{-\mathsf{N}}{-\mathsf{C}}{-\mathbf{X}}$ samples were not as readily apparent regarding to $\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0$ , which was associated with the generation of Fe- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf N_{x}}$ SAs.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization was performed to investigate the elemental composition and state of the elements on the surface of the representative catalyst $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\mathrm{N-}\mathsf{C}{-}0.75$ , which exhibited the best catalytic performance. As shown in the wide-scan spectrum (Fig. 6a), clear signals from C, N, O, and Fe elements could be detected, indicating their good existence in the catalyst. And this experimental result was consistent with the elemental mapping test (Fig. 2 d4). The specific C, N contents of the samples were determined by elemental analysis, and listed in Table 1. The high-resolution C 1 s XPS spectrum was showed in Fig. 6b. The C 1 s peaks could be divided into four single peaks centered at about 284.5, 285.4, 286.4, and $289.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ which were assigned to $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon, $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ carbon, C–O, and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}/$ $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond [36,37]. The spectrum of N 1 s (Fig. 6c) can be separated into five characteristic peaks with binding energies of 398.2, 399.1, 400.6, 401.5, and $403.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ that correspond to pyridinic-N, Fe–N, pyrrolic-N, graphitic-N, and oxidized-N, respectively [25,35,38,39]. The N species could serve as anchor sites for Fe atoms, and the presence of Fe–N peaks indicated the possible formation of SA dispersed Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ moiety, which were always regarded as the active sites [38,40,41]. The percentage of different types of N in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ samples obtained from XPS analysis were shown in Fig. S4. The percentage of Fe–N species in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}^{-}}\mathrm{N}_{-}$ C–0.75 was relatively higher than in other samples, which may influence the reaction effect. As shown in Fig. 6d, it revealed the corresponding Fe 2p spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ . The fitted peak at about $708~\mathrm{eV}$ was originated from the existence of $\mathsf{F e}^{0}$ And the peaks assigned to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ orbitals of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ were detected. As for the peaks at around $712.6~\mathrm{eV}$ , they could be associated with the chemical bond of Fe–N. In addition, the peak at about $717.8\mathrm{eV}$ was recognized as the shake-up satellite peak of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ species [34,42,43]. The loading of Fe in $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-}\mathrm{N-}$ $\operatorname{C-X}$ samples was determined by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES), and the detection values were summarized in Table 1.
Furthermore, the specific surface areas and pore structures of the prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}–\mathsf{N}–\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ samples were investigated by using the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption technique. As shown in Fig. 7a, it could be found that all the calcined samples presented type IV like isotherms with an obvious hysteresis loop in the 0.4–1.0 pressure $\left(\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}\right)$ range, indicating the existence of abundant mesopores [27,44,45]. The specific surface areas of the samples were calculated by adopting the Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) method. And the pore volumes and pore size distributions were measured by using Barrett–Joyner–Hallenda (BJH) model. The detailed parameter values of specific surface area, pore volume, and pore size were listed in Table 1. As for $\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0$ , which was derived from Fe-doped ZIF–0 without the addition of VC, it showed a much smaller specific surface area than that of other $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}{-\mathsf{N}}{-\mathsf{C}}{-\mathbf{X}}$ samples. These results further proved the importance of VC, which contributed a great deal in affecting the formation of the pore structures. The pore size distribution suggested the pores of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{C}{-}0$ were mainly concentrated in $2\ensuremath{-}3\ \ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ (Fig. 7b), commonly found in simple ZIF-derived samples. In comparison, the pores of other $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}-\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{X}$ samples were concentrated at about $7{-}8\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Interestingly, in all those calcined samples, $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N-C-}}0.75$ showed a relatively bigger specific surface area, which may facilitate the dispersion of active sites and promote the catalytic process.
Moreover, we compared the pore structure changes of Fe-doped ZIF–0.75, fresh $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ , and the reused $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-}\mathrm{N}{-}$ C–0.75 (the sample reused after five cycles) in Fig. S5. Most of the pores of Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 were micropores, which originated from the ZIF structure. In the calcination process, plenty of micropores were transformed into mesopores and even macropores with the aid of corrosive gases generated from the high-temperature pyrolysis of imidazole linkers and VC. In the meantime, the evaporation of Zn also did good to the formation of mesoporous and macroporous structures. The BET surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-$ 0.75 was determined to be $413~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was a little lower than Fe-doped ZIF–0.75 $\cdot458\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}.$ . When used after several times, the specific surface area of the recovered $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-C-$ 0.75 became smaller compared with the fresh catalyst, which may due to the fact that some substance got into the pore structures during the reaction.
## 3.2. Catalytic evaluation of the catalysts
