## 3.2.1. Screening of the optimal catalyst
Based on the above theoretical reaction equation and our previous work, firstly, in our reaction system, 0.5 mmol 4-CNB with excess amount of ${\ N}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ $\cdot2\ \mathrm{mmol};$ was added. Then each of the prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{X}$ (the Fe content relative to 4-CNB was $5.0\mathrm{\mol}\%$ ) was tested for the transfer hydrogenation under $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Surprisingly, all the samples could obtain $>99\%$ yield of 4- chlorobenzenamine (4-CBA) in $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Thus, in order to reflect the catalytic effect difference of different catalysts, we explored this reaction at room temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . As presented in Table 2, 4-CNB could be fully hydrogenated into 4-CBA using $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-N-}$ C–0.75 without dichlorination within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ (Table 2, entry 4), which exhibited the best catalytic performance remarkably. The yield of 4-CBA was only $46.0\%$ at $30\mathrm{min}$ , and $51.6\%$ at $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ over $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0$ (Table 2, entry 1). It was worth noting that no coupling intermediates were observed in the GC–MS spectrum and the 4-CBA selectivity was $>99\%$ for all cases. When adding
