## 3.2.2. Screening of the reaction conditions
Subsequently, we further explored the catalyst dosage and the adding amount of ${\ N}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ by using $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ as the representative catalyst, and the catalytic results were listed in Table 3. When no catalyst was added, just added with 2 mmol $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{H}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , only a tiny amount of 4-CBA was generated (Table 3, entry 1). When 5 mg $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ and $2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ reducing agent ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ were added, the yield of 4-CBA at 30 and $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ were $63.4\%$ and $90.3\%$ , respectively (Table 3, entry 2). When increased the catalyst dosage to $15\mathrm{mg}$ , the reaction rate was not significantly improved. It may be because there were already sufficient active sites to proceed the reaction with $10~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst (Table 3, entry 3). Combined with the previous reaction results (Table 2, entry 4), $10~\mathrm{mg}$ was considered to be the relatively appropriate catalyst dosage. Then, the addition amount of ${\mathsf{N}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ was also investigated. It was found that when only added $10~\mathrm{mg~Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}^{-}}\mathrm{N}{-}C{-}$ 0.75 without the addition of ${\mathsf{N}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ , no conversion of 4-CNB was observed. This reaction phenomenon reflected the importance of ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ as the hydrogen sources. When the adding amount of ${\ N}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ was 0.5 or $1\mathrm{mmol}$ , it was not enough for the complete conversion of 4-CNB. The reaction results showed that the 4-CBA yield of 1 mmol ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ was about more than twice of $0.5\mathrm{mmol}$ . When 4 mmol ${\sf N}_{2}{\sf H}_{4}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ was added, 4-CNB could be entirely converted to 4-CBA in 30 min. Based on the concept of green chemistry and atom economy, 2 mmol ${\sf N}_{2}{\sf H}_{4}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ was chosen as the proper reducing agent adding amount. To verify the importance of the metal species, the catalytic performance of free-metal catalysts N–C and N–C–0.75 were determined under the optimized reaction conditions. In the control tests, these two catalysts showed a lower yield of 4-CBA (Table 3, entries 8 and 9) than the metal-contained catalysts, indicating the metal components were the main active sites for the catalytic reaction. Meanwhile, the N species in the catalysts also played specifical catalytic effects and may combine with Fe species to catalyze the reaction.
## 3.3. Substrate scope
The general universality of a catalyst is of significant importance both in academic research and industrial application. In this regard, inspired by the good catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-N-}$ C–0.75, the catalytic transfer hydrogenation test of other different halogen-substituted nitrobenzenes was also conducted using $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N-C-}}0.75$ as the catalyst under the optimal reaction conditions. As shown in Table 4, all the halogen-substituted nitrobenzenes could be hydrogenated to corresponding halogenated anilines without dehalogenation. Although the hydrogenation of 4-bromonitrobenzene and 4-iodonitrobenzene needed a longer reaction time than other substrates, $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0.75$ showed relatively good hydrogenation performance. The above reaction results indicated the promising potential in the practical application of our catalytic system. To compare the superiority of our catalyst, the comparison of chemoselective hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzene with previously reported catalysts was shown in Table S1.
## 3.4. Study of the active sites
The identification of active sites in the catalyst is a challenging issue, as the catalyst contains various potential active centers, involving exposed metal/oxide particles, metal/oxide particles encapsulated or partially encapsulated in the carbon layer, SA dispersed metal species, and the N heteroatom doping groups in the support [14,26,40]. Thus, in order to gain further information of the active sites, acid-leaching tests using low-concentration $(2{\mathrm{~mol.L}}^{-1}.$ and high concentration $(8\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) HCl or $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ , and poisoning tests with different amounts of KSCN were performed with $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0.75$ .
## 3.4.1. Acid-leaching test
Here, first, we treated the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ catalyst with $2\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ HCl or ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solution for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $80~^{\circ}C$ . After the lowconcentration acid treatment, the Fe-based particles (Fe and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C)$ were removed, and these were confirmed by the XRD characterization (Fig. S6) of the acid-treated samples compared to the untreated $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ Due to the removal of some Fe species, it was worth noting that the peak of graphitic carbon showed a specific shift to the lower 2h degree after acid treatment, and the intensity of graphitic carbon became higher compared to the diffraction peak of the metallic part. The relatively stable iron nitride and SA Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ species were remained. When the lowconcentration acid-treated samples were subjected to the transfer hydrogenation of 4-CNB, the yield of 4-CBA decreased dramatically, as shown in Table 5. Although the applied $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ and HCl had the same concentration, the strong oxidation and corrosion of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ could cause damage to the catalyst. Thus, the $2\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ treated catalyst showed a lower yield. When $8\ \mathrm{\mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ was used to treat the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}-0.75$ , the highconcentration $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ could further remove the metal species, damage the structure of the material, and even etch the SA Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ moiety. As a result, the high-concentration ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ treated sample exhibited the worst catalytic performance. These acid-leaching test results showed that the Fe-based particles and the SA Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ species all had effects on the reactivity. However, the Fe-based particles (Fe and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C)$ may possess higher catalytic activity compared to the SA Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ species, and may play a bigger role in the reaction. Or, it may be the synergistic effects of all the species in the catalyst together that promoted the best catalytic performance towards the transfer hydrogenation reaction. All the species in the catalytic system were indispensable.
## 3.4.2. Poisoning test
It is well known that the ${\mathsf{S C N}}^{-}$ has a high affinity to Fe and can poison Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ species [26,39,40]. To further distinguish the catalytic effects of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ and Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ in the catalyst, KSCN was used to investigate the poisoning effect. During the hydrogenation process, different amounts of KSCN were added into the reaction system. As shown in Fig. 8, the KSCN introduced specific deactivation of the catalyst rather than completely inactivated. When the adding amount of KSCN was $10\%$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ dosage, the yield of 4-CBA decreased from $>99\%$ (when no KSCN was added, Table 2, entry 4) to $47.8\%$ at $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . In this situation, the sensitive $\mathsf{F e-N_{x}}$ species may be the main poisoned parts, and the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ with a relatively larger size may not be poisoned or partially poisoned. When the adding amount of KSCN increased to $20\%$ , the catalytic effect was further reduced. But when the adding amount of KSCN increased to a certain point, the catalytic performance did not show much difference, and their catalytic effects at $30~\mathrm{min}$ were equal to metal-free catalysts. The reason for these results may be attribute to the almost entire poisoning of the Fe species. Combining the poisoning and acid-leaching test results, it could be concluded that all the Fe species in the catalyst had contributed to the overall catalytic performance, and the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ may exhibit a better catalytic effect than Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ in our catalytic system.
