## 3.6. Stability of the FeSA/Fe NC–N–C–0.75
Finally, the stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ was evaluated. The transfer hydrogenation of 4-CNB by ${\sf N}_{2}{\sf H}_{4}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ was used as the model reaction. After each reaction, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ was recovered by centrifugation, washed with ethanol several times, and then used for the next catalytic run under identical conditions. It can be seen that the catalyst could still maintain a high yield of 4-CBA even after five successive cycle tests, indicating its good reusability (Table 6). And we also performed the filtration (room temperature $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{\cdot}$ ) and hot filtration $(60~^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ experiments. As our catalyst exhibited too high activity, we reduced the catalyst usage to $5\mathrm{mg}$ . The reaction results were shown in Fig. 11, and there was no subsequent reaction after separating the catalyst from the reaction system. In addition, ICP analysis showed the content of Fe was less than $0.3\mathrm{\ppm}$ in the filtrate. The super low leaching of Fe proved the stability of heterogeneous $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}{-\mathrm{N-C-}0.75}$ in a liquid phase reaction system. Moreover, the structure of the catalyst after five reactions was also characterized by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption measurements and XRD measurements, which were shown in Fig. S5 and Fig. S9. The XRD pattern of the reused $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/$ $\mathrm{Fe_{NC}}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N-C-}}0.75$ did not show obvious change, only with a small peak of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ compared with the fresh one. The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption– desorption resulsts revealed the surface area and pore volume of the reused catalyst decreased a little, which may be caused by the the occupation of some pores of the catalyst by some substance in the reaction solution. Thus, the the catalytic effect was affected to some extent. The results above showed the relatively good stability and reusability of the catalyst.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, a series of Fe-doped ZIF–x was synthesized by simply adjusting the adding amount of VC in one pot. After pyrolysis under inert atmosphere, $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ integrated with Fe single-atom planted in $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon $(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{S A}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathsf{N C}}–\mathsf{N}–\mathsf{C}-\mathbf{x})$ with various morphology, structure and composition could be precisely obtained. Among which, $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{SA}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{NC}}\mathrm{-N-C-}0.75$ showed the best catalytic performance for the transfer hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes under ambient conditions. Detailed experimental and DFT calculation results demonstrated that Fe single-atom and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}C$ jointly together could promote the catalyst to achieve better performance compared with separated Fe single-atom or $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ . This article may shed light on the rational design of high-performance catalysts with metal single-atom jointly with nanoclusters or nanoparticles, and could be a reference and guide for further study in the future.
